WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.167
Every provision of the US Code has an address — at every point in time it has existed.

2
00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:11.167
The front page lists the titles that are loaded.

3
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:15.293
The citation is the URL. This is 16 U.S.C. § 45f.

4
00:00:15.293 --> 00:00:20.767
The section, with its number and heading as the Code prints them.

5
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.066
Ask for subsection (c)(5) —

6
00:00:24.066 --> 00:00:30.400
— and it is highlighted inside the whole section, never stranded out of context.

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.385
Reading order is preserved, so you can move through a chapter section by section.

8
00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:38.769
The sticky bar carries previous, next and up-one-level.

9
00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:42.400
§ 45g — the next section, whatever you were reading before.

10
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:49.036
Add ?release= to any address to read it as it stood at that release point.

11
00:00:49.036 --> 00:00:55.133
The page names the release point it answered from — every page does.

12
00:00:55.133 --> 00:01:01.070
Or ask by date, and the site finds the release point in force then.

13
00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.767
12 July 2026 resolves to release point 119-102not101.

14
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:12.458
The version history: one entry per distinct text, not one per release point.

15
00:01:12.458 --> 00:01:19.767
Each entry says when that text first appeared, and what it stood unchanged through.

16
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:25.025
A redline between any two release points, in the reading text.

17
00:01:25.025 --> 00:01:32.233
Removed words struck through, added words underlined — the amendment, not the markup.

18
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:36.668
One box takes a citation or a phrase — you do not have to say which.

19
00:01:36.668 --> 00:01:39.864
Type a citation in more or less any written form.

20
00:01:39.864 --> 00:01:42.581
And it goes straight to the provision.

21
00:01:42.581 --> 00:01:46.233
16 U.S.C. § 45f — no need to know the URL scheme at all.

22
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:51.631
Anything that is not a citation searches the text of the Code.

23
00:01:51.631 --> 00:01:57.900
Matches are highlighted, and each result says where it sits in the Code.

24
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.327
Every provision has a copy control, and the whole section has its own button.

25
00:02:03.327 --> 00:02:07.556
Choose what copying means — text, citation, both, or a link.

26
00:02:07.556 --> 00:02:12.067
The page says what it copied, out loud, for a screen reader too.

27
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:16.689
Statutory text is full of references to other statutory text.

28
00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:20.326
Hover one and the cited provision comes to you —

29
00:02:20.326 --> 00:02:25.100
— heading, status and opening words, without losing your place.

30
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.852
Light by default at every OS setting — this is a reading site.

31
00:02:29.852 --> 00:02:33.045
One control in the header switches it.

32
00:02:33.045 --> 00:02:37.567
The choice is remembered, and lands before the page paints.
